    "result_text": "The entire test suite is green (261 tests, 0 failures/errors), including the new `AcceptanceTest.coreRejectsOverDailyLimit` and all existing tests.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the daily owner-creation limit for `POST /api/owners`, following the project's OfficeFloor function-injection conventions (mirroring the existing city-capacity guard):\n\n1. **`EnsureDailyOwnerLimit`** (function) \u2014 counts existing owners whose `registrationDate` equals today via `OwnerRepository.findAll()`, and throws when the count reaches 100. Runs before `BuildOwner`/`SaveOwner`, so the rejection happens instead of a persisted row.\n\n2. **`DailyOwnerLimitException`** (checked exception) \u2014 carries the limit constant `DAILY_OWNER_LIMIT = 100`.\n\n3. **`DailyOwnerLimitExceptionHandler`** \u2014 responds **429 (Too Many Requests)** with a standard `ProblemDetail` body.\n\n4. **Escalation YAML** `...DailyOwnerLimitException.yml` \u2014 wires the exception to its handler.\n\n5. **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 inserted an `ensureDailyLimit` step (governed by the same `transaction`) between the city-capacity guard and `build`.\n\nThe rule counts by `registrationDate` (which `BuildOwner` defaults to `LocalDate.now()`), so the 101st create in a day is rejected with 429, exactly as the acceptance test requires.",
